#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096459 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "b77c065c-3821-513c-9376-6e7108074616" :question/math-academy-id "e-4701" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n![](images/49/49df59f984403cbbc994361c96313eca4d0875a7030dd997c90f1e393e23b69c.png)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/dd/dded05f0961034de6808d8f91f64afe8c367cec3c11fafdb26946090fa9c0891.png)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=2$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(2)=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=-4$ and $z=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-4)+(2)=1\\,⟹\\,x=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-5,$ $y=-4,$ and $z=2.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -5,-4,2 \\rangle.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096460 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges"}] :question/id #uuid "7760b6ce-91fb-5ba6-beb2-c244d6a73d69" :question/math-academy-id "e-4702" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/0c/0c3483f97994da22dc0c739f9c3a26f87e070d28b684e63955a1129d00f9c755.png)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/9f/9f1beb75e17aabdbeb5d814ae7ce3018f2ea89c36211070d40060d57445ca936.png)\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get $a_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/84/84de3f5eddf0a05d4f2d69aebd1537feb23a228fba3e1af31678dd4bdb1b59df.png)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=1$ tells us the value of $z,$ and the second equation $y=3$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting these values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+(3)+(1)=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-6,$ $y=3,$ and $z=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -6,3,1 \\rangle.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efe06148-bbe9-5365-8632-2833be4456aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 1, 2, 5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093597} {:answer/id #uuid "fac5cd29-c335-599a-92ef-fef71dd3de34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6, 3, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093598} {:answer/id #uuid "0352ecec-0d9e-50c3-ae40-4b4dd34a0d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1, 2, 5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093599} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4f4cf9-1a04-59ce-ab56-da3904ce9b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 3, 1, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093600} {:answer/id #uuid "614239a4-1a3f-5b27-8496-eea4fa44e7b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6, 3, 5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093601}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb86e12-bfe4-5ef7-bae6-d878487ed96f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093596}] :question/id #uuid "6f82ff82-f58e-5dd1-b2bf-5cc0bd58f164" :question/math-academy-id "q-10676" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 25\\end{bmatrix}$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046f428e-556f-5956-b78b-d2844abad4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284826} {:answer/id #uuid "babfedb6-0dfd-59fe-83fc-1aade7621c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286487} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a979f3-bf84-5ce4-a382-9675dac8cb9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286562} {:answer/id #uuid "e3dfdeab-6885-5e75-a55d-ac0b26ea713b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286868} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ba7f8a-2143-5951-aadf-be80dd868963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "babfedb6-0dfd-59fe-83fc-1aade7621c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286487} :answer-field/id #uuid "78caa516-f3d8-59a7-8c65-740773cd1cf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a19bbe50-faec-58bf-b57a-44ae8f766527" :question/math-academy-id "q-20505" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y+6z=5 \\\\  & y-10z=-11 \\\\  & 4x+8y+13z=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 13 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 13 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{11}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9341f62-c8f8-570d-80c8-63278f533fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=13$, $y=4$, and $z=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368711} {:answer/id #uuid "cf00639b-8c94-5353-94da-3f2b856bf365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=13$, $y=6$, and $z=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368712} {:answer/id #uuid "48282e37-d5c2-509a-8252-6ee2a8e03b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-8$, $y=-3$, and $z=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368713} {:answer/id #uuid "3baa2212-ba77-51f2-be2f-5e043a5564f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=10$, $y=3$, and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368714} {:answer/id #uuid "da3d5251-f525-5757-9a3a-816f522e1513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=16$, $y=5$, and $z=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48282e37-d5c2-509a-8252-6ee2a8e03b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-8$, $y=-3$, and $z=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368713} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc8d2c28-de03-52ea-8fb5-2bfbbb958a9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f809106-1e50-5a91-813a-0a376c6d5c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-73587" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y=1 \\\\ y-z=-4 \\\\ z=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=1$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(1)=-4\\,⟹\\,y=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3(-3)=1\\,⟹\\,x=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-8$, $y=-3$, and $z=1.$"}]}
